WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.234 --> 00:00:01.767
-Our next guest is

00:00:01.767 --> 00:00:02.902
an Emmy-winning comedian

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:04.604
whose upcoming tour,
"Who's Ready to Laugh?",

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:05.905
kicks off September 10th

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:09.309
in Medford, Massachusetts,
at the Chevalier Theatre.

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:12.245
He also stars in "A.P. Bio,"
which returns to Peacock

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:14.314
for its fourth season
September 2nd.

00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:15.381
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:16.616
-Ralph, this is insane!

00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:17.950
Please cancel the uniforms.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.321
-That is a no, I can't do,
alright? Look.

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:23.089
I was talking
to the superintendent

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:25.491
and she said one
of the biggest problems

00:00:25.491 --> 00:00:27.360
in this district is bullying.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.627
So I pointed out, "Well,

00:00:28.627 --> 00:00:30.629
if everybody wore
the same thing,

00:00:30.629 --> 00:00:32.631
less stuff to make fun of,"
you know?

00:00:32.631 --> 00:00:36.936
Because it's harder to tell
when someone is...

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:39.873
dorky and who's...

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:42.141
poor.

00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:43.409
-Mm.
-They're going to call it

00:00:43.409 --> 00:00:46.645
the Durbin Anti-Bullying
Uniform Campaign.

00:00:46.645 --> 00:00:49.015
Yay!

00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:50.249
-Who will?
-Yay!

00:00:50.249 --> 00:00:52.051
-Who's going to call it that?

00:00:52.051 --> 00:00:53.820
[ Laughter ]
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.721
our good friend Patton Oswalt.

00:00:55.721 --> 00:00:57.223
How are you, Patton?

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:59.425
-I'm great.
Good to be back, Seth.

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:00.827
-This is very exciting --

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:03.829
you're about to embark
on a tour.

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:05.531
Like all stand-ups,

00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:06.899
I think you've been
on the shelf.

00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:09.468
Are you so excited
to get back out?

00:01:09.468 --> 00:01:11.203
-I am very, very excited.

00:01:11.203 --> 00:01:13.406
I'm a little bit nervous.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.341
It feels like maybe we're --

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:17.677
I hope we're not heading back
towards a shutdown.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:20.446
I'm making my touring plans
in good faith.

00:01:20.446 --> 00:01:24.883
But it almost feels like
we were so close

00:01:24.883 --> 00:01:27.386
to getting into the club.

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:28.755
Like the doorman,

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:30.389
our group of friends
was kind of schmoozing him.

00:01:30.389 --> 00:01:31.758
He's like, "I like these guys.

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:33.125
You know what?
Wait like ten minutes.

00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:34.159
I'll wait for 20 people
to leave.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:35.228
I'll get you guys in,"

00:01:35.228 --> 00:01:37.030
and then,
just before we're there,

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.464
one of the members
of our group goes,

00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:39.798
"Just let us in, [bleep]!"

00:01:39.798 --> 00:01:40.800
-[ Laughs ]
-He's like, "You know what?

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.068
All of you, no."

00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:46.038
Like that's how this second wave
of COVID feels like.

00:01:46.038 --> 00:01:49.576
The Delta variant is our friend
saying, "Let us in, [bleep]."

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:53.546
-Yes. I think that is
a very good analogy.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.115
And, you know, I think, you,
obviously, are in California,

00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:58.251
California's had
a very tough time of it,

00:01:58.251 --> 00:01:59.986
getting locked back down,
to some degree.

00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:01.954
Certainly, things are
coming back into effect.

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:03.556
How do you view the way

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:07.893
your fellow Californians
have approached this pandemic?

00:02:07.893 --> 00:02:09.629
-I mean, for the most part,
we've been good,

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.598
but, unfortunately,
California is home

00:02:12.598 --> 00:02:16.502
to a lot of people on the very
far end of the health spectrum,

00:02:16.502 --> 00:02:20.272
who are very much about,
"I care what goes into my body

00:02:20.272 --> 00:02:22.408
and I don't feel like
this vaccine

00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:24.443
is part of a paleo lifestyle."

00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:27.547
So, whereas, you have people
in the Midwest saying,

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:30.616
[ Changes accent ]
"That needle's got
which poison in it,"

00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:34.787
-[ Laughs ]
-they're weirdly aligned
with the yoga freaks going,

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:37.256
"You know, if we could all
just align our chakras

00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:40.660
at the same time, the virus
would go to a different planet."

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.029
[ Laughter ]
So it's equally bad.

00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:45.565
It's just
on a different wavelength.

00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:47.833
-You know, again, we're talking
about how things are.

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:50.503
They did, at least, seem like
they were going back to normal,

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:52.338
but not necessarily
in a positive way.

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.306
There were some things
that changed that,

00:02:54.306 --> 00:02:57.009
it maybe would've been better
if it stayed that way.

00:02:57.009 --> 00:03:00.746
-Yes. Okay, and I remember this
very clearly.

00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:02.214
Right when
the shutdown happened,

00:03:02.214 --> 00:03:04.083
right when the
quarantine happened,

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:05.785
and I was one of them, saying,

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:08.454
"Well, cruise ships
and buffets are gone.

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:10.389
They're never coming back
after this,"

00:03:10.389 --> 00:03:11.758
and I'm realizing

00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:14.727
buffets are going to come back
with a vengeance

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:17.363
and they're going
to come back with this weird

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:20.966
like super patriot MAGA tinge
to them, going, you know --

00:03:20.966 --> 00:03:22.135
[ Changes accent ]
"Are you one of those

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:24.570
left coast liberals
that needs a sneeze guard

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.305
over their clam chowder,

00:03:26.305 --> 00:03:28.641
or are you able
to [bleep] your wife?"

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:30.076
[ Laughter ]
That's how they're going

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:32.411
to advertise buffets
from now on.

00:03:32.411 --> 00:03:36.382
It's all going to have
this weird MAGA twist to them.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.552
-Very excited about Season 4
of "A.P. Bio."

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:42.922
Principal Durbin, a perfect
character for Patton Oswalt.

00:03:42.922 --> 00:03:46.626
But I also wanted to look back
at some really good photos

00:03:46.626 --> 00:03:50.262
-[ Laughing ] Oh, God.
-of you from school growing up.

00:03:50.262 --> 00:03:53.232
This is in line with, I think,
both how you present yourself

00:03:53.232 --> 00:03:54.600
and maybe
how people see yourself.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.836
Of course,
you were involved in D&amp;D.

00:03:56.836 --> 00:03:59.972
-Of course.

00:03:59.972 --> 00:04:01.474
-Of the people that are
in this photo,

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:04.443
you are the most D&amp;D person
in this photo.

00:04:04.443 --> 00:04:05.878
-The most.

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:09.882
I am holding the original
hardback player's manual.

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:13.452
I look like I've just been
given Aerosmith tickets,

00:04:13.452 --> 00:04:16.723
[ Laughter ]
I'm so happy in that photo.

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:19.258
I don't think I've ever
been that happy.

00:04:19.258 --> 00:04:20.460
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:21.760
-I could not be more nerded out.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.331
I got the weird little glasses
and -- oh, my God. Mwah!

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:26.999
-Everything about it.
Like, yeah,

00:04:26.999 --> 00:04:29.202
you feel like genuinely so happy
that they were like,

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:30.269
"You guys want
to get a picture?"

00:04:30.269 --> 00:04:31.503
You're like, "Yeah!

00:04:31.503 --> 00:04:34.339
[ Laughter ]
I want people to know this!"

00:04:34.339 --> 00:04:36.308
-Of the whole group,
you can tell that I was the one

00:04:36.308 --> 00:04:37.643
that ran over, went, "Hang on!"

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:39.645
And I grabbed my player's manual

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:41.914
to like hold it up like,
"I've got it!"

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:43.683
[ Laughter ]
-Now, I didn't know this,

00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:45.218
but you would argue --
because this is you

00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:47.119
doing something in college.

00:04:47.119 --> 00:04:49.822
This is an athletic activity.

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:54.893
But you see this as a natural
throughline connected to D&amp;D.

00:04:54.893 --> 00:04:56.996
-Yes.
-Tell us what you're doing here.

00:04:56.996 --> 00:05:00.967
-I was on the fencing team
my first two years of college.

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:04.237
That's me --
third from the left.

00:05:04.237 --> 00:05:06.138
And, in my mind,
that was a straight line

00:05:06.138 --> 00:05:09.308
from all the years of imagining
wielding a sword to,

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:12.111
"Okay, I'm at college and I
can actually wield a sword."

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:14.246
[ Laughter ]
And so there I was with my foil.

00:05:14.246 --> 00:05:17.083
I tried for saber,
did not have the arm speed,

00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:19.252
and then, by sophomore year,

00:05:19.252 --> 00:05:21.754
I had discovered weed
and the Beastie Boys

00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:25.124
and, in a weird way, that's also
part of the same trajectory.

00:05:25.124 --> 00:05:26.392
[ Laughter ]

00:05:26.392 --> 00:05:27.392
So, yeah.

00:05:27.392 --> 00:05:29.361
-You feel like,

00:05:29.361 --> 00:05:31.697
look, I go to a lot
of fencing meets.

00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:34.533
You never hear Beastie Boys
over the PA.

00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:37.669
-No, you do not.
-I feel like those two
do not mesh well.

00:05:37.669 --> 00:05:38.871
-And, actually,
now that I think of it,

00:05:38.871 --> 00:05:41.006
there are some really good
Beastie Boys songs

00:05:41.006 --> 00:05:43.075
that would be great
walkout songs for fencers,

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:44.910
now that I think I said it.
-100%.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:47.346
-Yeah. "Looking
Down the Barrel of a Gun"

00:05:47.346 --> 00:05:50.215
would be a great walkout song
as you unsheathe your sword

00:05:50.215 --> 00:05:51.550
and they do that whole rolling.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.452
That'd be amazing.
Off of "Paul's Boutique."

00:05:53.452 --> 00:05:55.287
Great walkout song for fencers.

00:05:55.287 --> 00:05:58.524
-If you're a young fencer
watching the show right now,

00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:00.425
take Patton's word for it.

00:06:00.425 --> 00:06:03.328
You have a podcast
with your lovely wife, Meredith.

00:06:03.328 --> 00:06:07.433
-I have a podcast with my wife,
called "Did You Get My Text?"

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:10.435
And it's just so happens
-Oh, do we have her here?

00:06:10.435 --> 00:06:11.904
-that my lovely wife
-Hi!

00:06:11.904 --> 00:06:13.772
-is sitting right here!
-Hello!

00:06:13.772 --> 00:06:15.308
Nice to see you, Meredith.

00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:18.444
-Wait. Seth, thank you
for showing that picture

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:20.079
of Patton playing D&amp;D.
-Oh, gotcha.

00:06:20.079 --> 00:06:22.081
No, not that picture.
-Yeah, this one.

00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:25.351
-Because I cannot tell you
how much more in love with him

00:06:25.351 --> 00:06:27.319
that that makes me.
That is crazy.

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:29.522
[ Laughter ]
-Wow!

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:31.290
-Are you at all concerned
that he's never seemed

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:32.758
that happy in your presence?

00:06:32.758 --> 00:06:34.727
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.396
-I mean, that is the cutest
thing I've ever seen in my life.

00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:39.732
It makes me substantially
more attracted to you.

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:41.200
I did not think that
that picture

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.102
would be catnip for hotties.

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:44.403
I had no idea.

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:47.539
-So, alright, so this, guys,
"Did You Get My Text?"

00:06:47.539 --> 00:06:49.876
This is a podcast
about your relationship.

00:06:49.876 --> 00:06:52.345
You might think this is about
a long-distance relationship

00:06:52.345 --> 00:06:55.915
because it is about your
texting communication,

00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:57.950
but that is absolutely
not the case.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.987
-No. We are a married couple
who lives together

00:07:00.987 --> 00:07:02.955
and I think,
like a lot of married couples

00:07:02.955 --> 00:07:06.425
who've gotten a lot of feedback,
we are in the same house,

00:07:06.425 --> 00:07:08.294
but we text each other all day.

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:09.362
-Yeah.
He's in his room.

00:07:09.362 --> 00:07:10.896
I'm in my part of the house

00:07:10.896 --> 00:07:13.265
and it's just easier
to text each other.

00:07:13.265 --> 00:07:15.635
But we don't really ever
sit down and talk about it,

00:07:15.635 --> 00:07:17.737
so the podcast sort of
is an opportunity for us

00:07:17.737 --> 00:07:19.805
to sit down
and talk to each other.

00:07:19.805 --> 00:07:21.707
Not just give a thumbs-up
on something he said,

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:24.110
but like communicate.
-"What was this?

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:27.246
Why did you send this
and what was this?"

00:07:27.246 --> 00:07:28.714
-That's interesting.

00:07:28.714 --> 00:07:31.083
My wife and I have talked
about the thumbs-up,

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:33.385
that sometimes that is not --

00:07:33.385 --> 00:07:37.456
It seems like
a passive-aggressive approval,

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:38.691
that you would do the thumbs-up,

00:07:38.691 --> 00:07:40.859
as opposed to writing,
"That sounds great."

00:07:40.859 --> 00:07:42.995
-Yeah.
Or it also feels like,

00:07:42.995 --> 00:07:44.497
"Okay, conversation's over.

00:07:44.497 --> 00:07:47.099
[ Laughter ]
Like got it. Moving on."
-Right, yeah.

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:49.869
Which is also
passive-aggressive, you know?

00:07:49.869 --> 00:07:52.204
-Yeah.
-Well, it's also because you --

00:07:52.204 --> 00:07:54.774
I haven't given a physical
thumbs-up in a long time.

00:07:54.774 --> 00:07:57.510
So if my wife said to me like,
you know,

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:58.911
"You want to have a dinner
with my friends tonight?"

00:07:58.911 --> 00:08:00.846
And I did that,
I think she'd be like,

00:08:00.846 --> 00:08:02.281
"You know, get over yourself."

00:08:02.281 --> 00:08:04.316
[ Laughter ]

00:08:04.316 --> 00:08:07.520
-And also, that would seem
beyond rude.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:10.189
Someone gives you a proposal
and you just go, "Good."

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:11.490
[ Laughter ]

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:12.725
That'd be so awkward.

00:08:12.725 --> 00:08:15.327
-The texting situation
has gotten a little --

00:08:15.327 --> 00:08:16.828
-Oh, man.
-a little --

00:08:16.828 --> 00:08:19.932
It's a lot because sometimes,
like the other night,

00:08:19.932 --> 00:08:22.868
we were in bed together and our
backs were towards each other

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:24.937
and I was so comfortable

00:08:24.937 --> 00:08:26.171
on my side.
-She had found

00:08:26.171 --> 00:08:27.340
-I was like the perfect
-a perfect position...

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:29.007
-position, you know?
-...like, "Here I am."

00:08:29.007 --> 00:08:31.176
And then he's like, "Oh,
I want to show you something,"

00:08:31.176 --> 00:08:34.313
and I was like, "Ugh.
Can you just --"

00:08:34.313 --> 00:08:35.881
I just got this great position.

00:08:35.881 --> 00:08:37.850
-She did not want to turn over.

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:39.118
-Yeah, I didn't want
to turn over to look at it,

00:08:39.118 --> 00:08:40.886
so I was like,
"Can you just text it to me?"

00:08:40.886 --> 00:08:43.522
-So you were physically
touching and, yet,

00:08:43.522 --> 00:08:45.925
you needed to receive a text?
-In bed back-to-back.

00:08:45.925 --> 00:08:49.295
-And she had got the pillows
just how she wanted them

00:08:49.295 --> 00:08:52.231
and I found this cool thing
I wanted her to look at.

00:08:52.231 --> 00:08:54.166
And she said it with this
-I was laughing,

00:08:54.166 --> 00:08:55.534
-laughing,
but also knowing how --

00:08:55.534 --> 00:08:57.769
She goes, "You're going to need
to text that to me

00:08:57.769 --> 00:08:59.972
because I will not lose
this position."

00:08:59.972 --> 00:09:01.640
-I was so comfortable.
-I appreciate that.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:05.177
And I actually do think
you did Patton a favor

00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:08.680
because then it puts so much
less weight on the text

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:10.315
because, if you had to get
out of your position

00:09:10.315 --> 00:09:12.384
and Patton was showing you
something stupid,

00:09:12.384 --> 00:09:14.052
then how mad
you would've been at him.

00:09:14.052 --> 00:09:15.688
-"That's not that good,
for me to turn around.

00:09:15.688 --> 00:09:16.755
-You're right.
-It's not good enough."

00:09:16.755 --> 00:09:18.658
-What if the text
hadn't lived up

00:09:18.658 --> 00:09:20.826
to her losing this position?

00:09:20.826 --> 00:09:22.728
Wow!
-Right.
-Yeah.

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:23.862
-That makes a lot of sense.
-I think you guys

00:09:23.862 --> 00:09:25.331
have figured it out.

00:09:25.331 --> 00:09:26.666
-[Both] Yeah.
-We really have.

00:09:26.666 --> 00:09:28.834
-It's so lovely
to see both of you.

00:09:28.834 --> 00:09:30.902
It's so nice to see your faces

00:09:30.902 --> 00:09:32.471
and, hopefully, next time,
we'll see you in person.

00:09:32.471 --> 00:09:35.340
The fourth season
of "A.P. Bio" premieres

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:36.609
on Peacock September 2nd

00:09:36.609 --> 00:09:38.544
and the podcast
"Did You See My Text?"

00:09:38.544 --> 00:09:40.880
releases new episodes
every Tuesday.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.815
And one last time,
in case you're curious,

00:09:42.815 --> 00:09:45.617
that is how happy he was
to take that picture.

00:09:45.617 --> 00:09:47.386
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

